# REDACTED AND TRUNCATED.
#
# This probe's raw output was 900 KB and contained the CONTENTS of the
# operator's real ~/.claude, ~/.codex and ~/.gemini skill trees, because the
# mutation redirects `agentDirsOf` at them and the fixture's own byte-snapshot
# then read every file it was pointed at. Lines are truncated to 240 characters.
#
# THAT IS THE PROBE'S FINDING, not an accident of it: with the agent axis taken
# from the ENVIRONMENT rather than from the home, `home repair` run against a
# temp-directory home READ 60 of the operator's global agent skill links and
# reported all 60 as damaged.
#
# NOTHING WAS WRITTEN. Verified immediately after the run:
#   find ~/.skill-manager ~/.claude ~/.codex ~/.gemini <project>/.skill-manager \
#        -maxdepth 3 -newermt "-60 minutes"   ->   no output
#   every link under ~/.claude/skills, ~/.codex/skills, ~/.gemini/skills still
#   resolves into ~/.skill-manager/skills (issue #159's own detection snippet,
#   run by hand)
# and the reason is visible in the finding text above: `and this home does not
# hold skills/<unit>`, so every one of the 60 was reported UNREPAIRABLE and
# `repair` skipped it.
#
# READ THAT AS A WARNING RATHER THAN AS A SECOND GUARD. It held because the
# fixture home happened to hold none of those units. Under this mutation the
# WriteConfinement scope moves too -- it is derived from the same
# `agentDirsOf` -- so confinement would NOT have refused a write. The
# structural derivation of the agent axis from the store path is the only thing
# standing between `home repair --fix` and the operator's global links, which is
# why that assertion is the one this ticket cares most about.

# V7 -- branch moved: the AGENT-AXIS DERIVATION: HIS-14's defect, planted in the repairer.
